# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — AWS CDK infrastructure

TypeScript AWS CDK v2 project generated by the `cdk-setup` agent skill.

## Requirements

- Node.js {{NODE_ENGINE}} (LTS line recommended)
- {{PACKAGE_MANAGER}} (the `packageManager` field pins it; `corepack enable` makes it automatic)
- AWS credentials only for `diff` / `deploy` / `bootstrap` — never for `check`

## Commands

```bash
{{PM}} install          # install dependencies (lock file keeps versions reproducible)
{{PM}} run check        # format:check -> lint -> build -> test -> synth -> nag
{{PM}} run test         # vitest: snapshot + fine-grained CDK assertions
{{PM}} run synth        # synthesize all stacks into cdk.out/
{{PM}} run nag          # synth with cdk-nag AwsSolutionsChecks, warnings treated as errors
{{PM}} run diff         # cdk diff (needs credentials, read-only)
```

`{{PM}} run check` is the single validation entry point. It needs no AWS account, so it works the
same on a laptop and in any CI platform.

## Deployment

Deployment is a human action. Verify who you are, review the diff, then deploy:

```bash
aws sts get-caller-identity --profile <profile>
npx cdk diff {{PROJECT_NAME}}-{{DEFAULT_ENVIRONMENT}} --profile <profile>
npx cdk deploy {{PROJECT_NAME}}-{{DEFAULT_ENVIRONMENT}} --profile <profile>
```

A new account/region needs `cdk bootstrap aws://<account>/<region>` once. It creates IAM roles and
an S3 bucket, so run it yourself and read the change set.

## Structure

```text
bin/app.ts                  entry point (thin: calls createApp)
lib/app.ts                  app wiring: one stack per environment + cdk-nag aspect
lib/config/environments.ts  deployment targets (account/region) — configuration, not credentials
lib/stacks/                 deployment boundaries
lib/constructs/application/ constructs for this project's domain
lib/constructs/patterns/    reusable infrastructure patterns
test/                       snapshot + fine-grained assertions
```

Model with constructs, deploy with stacks: put complexity into constructs, and split stacks only for
a real deployment boundary (independent release, lifecycle, account, region, owner, blast radius).

## Environments

`lib/config/environments.ts` holds `{{ENVIRONMENT_LIST}}`. Accounts start unset, which makes the
stacks environment-agnostic so `synth`, tests and cdk-nag run without credentials. Fill in the
account IDs before deploying; that also enables context lookups (VPC, AMI, hosted zone).

Credentials stay outside the repository: IAM Identity Center, a CLI profile, AssumeRole, or CI OIDC.
Never commit an access key, and never hard-code a profile name in CDK code.

## Conventions

- Security defaults: private, encrypted, least privilege, logging where it helps. Do not weaken a
  default to make a tool pass — change it because the project decided to.
- IAM: prefer `grant*` APIs, then service-specific APIs, then explicit `PolicyStatement`. Wildcards
  need a written reason.
- cdk-nag findings are discussed, not suppressed. A suppression needs a scope and an audit reason.
- Snapshot tests are mandatory; add fine-grained assertions for loops, conditionals, props-driven
  values and security-relevant properties.
- Snapshots are committed. Read every snapshot diff — replacement and deletion hide there.

## Notes

- `cdk.json` holds the feature flags the installed CDK version recommends for a new project (taken
  from `cdk init` at setup time). Changing a flag on a deployed stack can change or replace
  resources — treat it as a deliberate, reviewed change.
- The default is an empty stack. `--starter queue` adds a queue and a dead-letter queue so cdk-nag
  has something real to evaluate; adapt or delete that construct — with its tests — once real
  infrastructure lands.
